        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "327bbb71-f838-5a5a-bc94-023cd85151dc",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Podocarpus, Osmunda, Anemia\n- Animals: Golden-plumed parakeet\n- Concepts: Podocarpaceae, flowering ferns\n\nFacts:\n- Podocarpus is a genus of conifers in the podocarp family Podocarpaceae.\n- Podocarpus is the most numerous and widely distributed genus in Podocarpaceae.\n- Podocarpus includes approximately 97 to 107 species, depending on species circumscription.\n- Podocarpus plants are evergreen shrubs or trees that are usually 1 to 25 m tall and can sometimes reach 40 m.\n- Osmunda includes five to ten listed species.\n- Species of Anemia are sometimes called flowering ferns.\n- The term flowering ferns is more commonly applied to ferns of the genus Osmunda.\n- The golden-plumed parakeet is found in humid temperate highland forests, especially forests with Podocarpus, on the east Andean slope in Colombia, Ecuador, and Peru.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "d2b39696-0506-5b01-bf1d-0c30c785817b",
        "text": "Page: Podocarpus. Sentence: Podocarpus ( ) is a genus of conifers, the most numerous and widely distributed of the podocarp family, Podocarpaceae.\nPage: Podocarpus. Sentence: \"Podocarpus\" are evergreen shrubs or trees, usually from 1 to tall, known to reach 40 m at times.\nPage: Podocarpus. Sentence: There are approximately 97 to 107 species in the genus depending on the circumscription of the species.\nPage: Osmunda. Sentence: Five to ten species have been listed for this genus.\nPage: Golden-plumed parakeet. Sentence: This somewhat \"Aratinga\"-like species is found in humid temperate highland forests, especially with \"Podocarpus\", on the east Andean slope in Colombia, Ecuador, and Peru.\nPage: Anemia (plant). Sentence: Species of the genus Anemia are sometimes called flowering ferns, but this term is more commonly applied to ferns of the genus \"Osmunda\".",
